faq:
  - q: What is psychodynamic therapy?
    a: Psychodynamic therapy explores how unconscious processes, early relationships and recurring relational patterns shape the way you feel and behave today.
  - q: How long does psychodynamic therapy take?
    a: It is a longer process than short-term approaches. Focused psychodynamic work may progress over roughly 16–30 sessions, while open-ended therapy can continue for a year or more.
  - q: Who is psychodynamic therapy suitable for?
    a: It suits people facing recurring relationship difficulties, long-standing low mood, questions of identity and self-worth, unresolved grief, or a sense of emptiness they cannot quite name.
  - q: Do I have to talk about my childhood?
    a: No. Most of the time we start from the present. The past enters when it is needed to understand the present, and when you are ready. You decide what to share and when.
